[{:db/id "2034e111-3281-5462-b0b0-a7ccb1a7c133" :answer/id #uuid "2034e111-3281-5462-b0b0-a7ccb1a7c133" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1 -x" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "8d9a6db0-05c1-5d85-8a67-056821eb3220" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d9a6db0-05c1-5d85-8a67-056821eb3220" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["2034e111-3281-5462-b0b0-a7ccb1a7c133"] :answer-field/correct "2034e111-3281-5462-b0b0-a7ccb1a7c133" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "e6fe3232-91eb-5c5b-90f0-5931f279e031" :question/id #uuid "e6fe3232-91eb-5c5b-90f0-5931f279e031" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,3)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-1(x-0) \\\\ y-1 & =-x \\\\ y & =1 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["8d9a6db0-05c1-5d85-8a67-056821eb3220"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "generator" :question-generator/id #uuid "d4f8e14e-e664-5d3c-b8e6-ce7ab96b3db1" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186175923.py"} [:db/add 17592186175923 :knowledge-point/question-generator "generator"] {:db/id 17592186175923 :know